title: Linux Bubblewrap Provider
status: implemented
created: 2026-11-07
updated: 2026-11-10
author: adrian
context: "The map at `maps/2026-10-31-nix-devshell-sandbox` chose bubblewrap for Linux and chose a holder process to keep the sandbox alive. Every fact this specification rests on is an answered ticket on that map, and each one has evidence beside it. T-003 answers whether bwrap can sandbox without root. It can, because it builds the sandbox from unprivileged user namespaces and needs no daemon. T-004 and T-016 answer whether a build works with the store bound read-only. It does, with an environment captured on the host. T-005 and T-012 answer what the sandbox can reach. The network namespace has loopback only, and each invocation gets its own pid and network context. T-013 answers whether a process can enter a live sandbox. It can, by setns, as the same uid and without privileges. That probe also established four details this ADR depends on. The pid to address is the child pid bwrap reports on `--json-status-fd`, not the pid the caller started. Killing pid 1 of the sandbox kills the whole pid namespace, including a command that called setsid. `setgroups` fails inside bwrap's user namespace, which is why `nsenter` needs `--preserve-credentials`. And the join should happen in the calling process, as Flatpak does it, not by running `nsenter`. Two kernel rules shape the join, and T-013 exercised both. setns into a pid namespace moves only the children created afterwards, never the process that called it. chroot does not close an inherited descriptor. T-024 answers whether the sandbox survives the command that started it. It does, with or without `--die-with-parent`, and the backend must detach its standard streams. T-019 answers how a pause covers commands that joined later. Each command is written into the sandbox's cgroup scope before it runs. cgroup freeze is the only atomic pause, and `cgroup.kill` is the atomic teardown. A single pass over the pid namespace leaks, because a process can fork between the scan and the signal. The stop-and-rescan loop is correct, but a peer running as the same uid can livelock it. Freeze and limits manage resources. They are not a boundary, because delegation lets a workload write its own cgroup.procs. T-009 and T-010 answer what a transient user scope gives. It gives freeze, thaw, MemoryMax and TasksMax, and no probe detects that a user manager is missing. T-022 answers whether the bind set can come from the closure. It can, and 238 paths ran the same test as the whole store. T-008 answers where user namespaces are available. NixOS and Fedora allow them. Ubuntu 24.04 blocks them until the AppArmor and clone sysctls are relaxed, and it ships bwrap 0.9.0, so the floor stays at 0.8.0 and the descriptor bind is a path bind of `/proc/self/fd/N`. T-018 answers whether nix can run inside the sandbox. It can, and it should not, so nix stays on the host. The contract this backend implements is `crates/agentkit-litterbox/src/sandbox/contract.rs`. The trait is `Backend` at line 547. The facets are at `src/sandbox/facets.rs:37-61`. `Contributions` is at line 169, `ResolvedStep` at 224, `CreateRequest` at 251 and `ContributionSupport` at 119. `Selected::standard_capabilities` at `src/sandbox/selected.rs:70` reports a facet as available, so a backend that degrades has to replace that entry. The environment capture is `src/provision/prepare/environment.rs`, and the closure is `src/provision/prepare/closure.rs`, which materialises whatever `nix path-info -r` printed against a store root the configuration chooses. Docker is the only production backend, at `src/backends/registry.rs:20`, and it reports no contribution support, at `src/backends/docker/backend.rs:723-725`. Nothing today can apply a devShell's environment or closure. Two registry tests use `bubblewrap` as the name of a backend that is not compiled in, and both have to move. The isolation prototype at `adrs/2026-10-31-linux-bwrap-sandbox/poc/` is the oracle for the view and the renderer, and its findings are in `adrs/2026-10-31-linux-bwrap-sandbox/findings.md`. That prototype validated every grant and refused protected roots, and this ADR keeps those rules rather than inventing new ones.\n\n## Probed\n\nThe questions this ADR would otherwise have carried as assumptions were probed on NixOS, kernel 7.2.15, bubblewrap 0.12.0, and the scripts and output are beside the map's evidence. T-033: the host's `sleep` takes a large integer duration and holds pid 1, so no GNU spelling is needed. T-034: a client killed while its command runs leaves no zombie, because the command is reparented outside the pid namespace and reaped there. T-035: 1779 individual binds reach a usable view in 4.2s and run a command, with one mount entry for each path. T-036: all seven namespace inodes stay identical across commands, so a record can check them. T-037: this host has cgroup v2, a user manager and a scope that starts. T-038: the cgroup namespace can be joined unprivileged, so a command shares all seven namespaces. T-039: the status document arrives long before the view is built, so create has to wait for the view rather than for the document. T-040: the last recorded mount appearing is the moment the view becomes usable. T-041: the namespaces are owned by the user namespace that is the parent of the sandbox's own, so the join enters that parent first and the sandbox's user namespace last.\n\n{{mermaid: lifecycle.mmd}}"
problem: "Litterbox can only sandbox through Docker. Docker reports no contribution support, so a project that declares a devshell step is refused before create, and the environment and closure that the capture produces have no consumer. A host without a Docker daemon has no mechanism at all, which is the case the map exists to reach. The contract has one implementation, so its contribution model, its lifecycle, its readiness report and its failure behaviour have only ever been exercised by tests. A second mechanism is also the first real test of the boundary. It has to validate every path it is given, because the existing backend receives paths from an image it builds itself. It also has to work for a project with no provisioning steps, where nothing contributes a shell, a PATH or any userland, and a command given to a tool still has to be evaluated."
goals[10]:
  - "Add a bubblewrap backend that implements the existing contract, which the person running the code selects, leaving Docker's behaviour and the default as they are."
  - "Create a holder that runs in seven new namespaces and outlives the command line tool."
  - "Run every command as a join into the holder, in the calling process, and make the command a real member of the sandbox pid namespace."
  - "Render the contributions as an environment, a set of read-only binds and a writable workspace."
  - "Give a sandbox with no provisioning steps a baseline, so a command given to a tool still runs."
  - "Give pause, resume, limits and delete a meaning at the sandbox level, and allow creation without cgroups only when the user accepts the weaker mode."
  - "Report readiness for bwrap, user namespaces, cgroup delegation, the holder command and the shell, with a reason and a remedy for each."
  - "Record only what a later process needs to join, check it before every use, and never join a process that this backend did not create."
  - "Refuse rather than degrade on any unsafe condition, and keep nix outside the sandbox."
  - "Test the renderer, the record, readiness and the join, including the cases that must fail, and document the configuration."
nonGoals[9]:
  - "The egress proxy and any filtering by destination. This ADR gives the sandbox loopback and nothing else."
  - "The Darwin backend and any renderer shared with it."
  - "Automatic backend selection. The user selects bubblewrap."
  - "Contribution support in Docker. Docker keeps reporting the sorts unsupported."
  - "Relaxing `--disable-userns` for a workload that creates its own user namespaces."
  - "A root daemon, a setuid helper or a system service. The backend runs as the user who invoked it."
  - "A survey of where cgroup v2 is available. Readiness reports what this host offers, and the degraded mode covers the rest."
  - "A package manager inside the sandbox. The baseline is a shell and the utilities the tools already call."
  - "Snapshot, command line and MCP work beyond what the contract already does, and the documentation the roadmap owns. Only this backend's configuration reference is in scope."
requirements[20]{id,title,slug,description}:
  FR-001,"Registration",backend-registration,"The registry gets a second entry named bubblewrap, with a constructor that reads its own section of the machine-owned configuration. DEFAULT stays docker, so a project or command that selects nothing behaves as it does today. A person selects the backend with `backend = 'bubblewrap'` in the user configuration or `--backend bubblewrap`. A project file cannot select it or change the settings that define its boundary, so no repository can ask for the weaker mechanism. A project that does not list it in `[project] backends` is refused before create, as it already is for Docker. One module beside the backend declares the section's keys, and an unknown key fails as Misconfigured and names the key. The backend is compiled on Linux only. Two registry tests use bubblewrap as the name of a backend that is not compiled in, and they move to a name no backend will use. A test asserts the entry exists on Linux and is absent elsewhere."
  FR-002,"Holder: Create",holder-create,"create starts one holder and returns a record that addresses it. If it cannot, it returns a failure and no sandbox exists. The holder is two processes. The launcher is the bwrap process the tool starts, and it runs in the caller's namespaces. The sandbox process is the pid bwrap reports as its child, and it is pid 1 of the new pid namespace. bwrap creates user, mount, pid, net, ipc, uts and cgroup namespaces and runs one command there for an unbounded time. The backend resolves that command on the host, validates it and binds it read-only. The sandbox has a private root, proc, dev, a temporary filesystem and a home. It is detached. It does not inherit standard input, output or error, it holds no terminal or session, it has no parent-death tie, and nested user namespaces are blocked. The sandbox therefore outlives the tool, as a branch does. The launcher starts with a cleared environment that holds exactly what the sandbox is to receive, so no variable from the caller's environment reaches it. create returns a sandbox only after the view is built. bwrap must have reported the sandbox pid and the namespace inodes, and every recorded mount must be present at its recorded path with its recorded access. A missing report is a create failure, and so is a view that does not complete within the provisioning budget (T-039). A create whose name already has a state directory, or a live sandbox, fails as Conflict before any process starts. Sandbox steps run after the sandbox exists, in sequence order, through the same join path as a command, and under the same budget and output cap as Docker. A step that exits non-zero fails the create and kills the sandbox."
  FR-003,"Command: Join",exec-join,"A command runs inside the sandbox and does not start a second sandbox. Before it joins anything, the backend opens the seven namespace descriptors and a descriptor for the sandbox root, because joining the mount namespace replaces `/proc`. The child joins the user namespace that owns the other namespaces first. That namespace is the parent of the sandbox's own user namespace, and the backend reaches it through the namespace descriptor rather than by path. The child then joins ipc, net, pid, mnt, uts and cgroup, and finally the sandbox's own user namespace. Joining the sandbox's user namespace first is not enough. The process gains capabilities there and none in the namespace that owns the others, so every later join fails with EPERM (T-041). setns into a pid namespace affects only children created later, so the child forks again and the grandchild runs the command. The grandchild closes every descriptor above standard error, chroots through the root descriptor it opened before joining, because the path stops resolving once the mount namespace is the sandbox's, changes to the workspace, sets no-new-privileges, takes the caller's already-mapped uid and gid, clears every capability, and execs the command with an environment built from the sandbox's own. It never calls setgroups, because bwrap denies it in the sandbox's user namespace, and it never writes allow to a setgroups file. Before exec the grandchild reads its own `/proc/self/ns` entries and compares each inode with the record. A difference fails the join, so a join that missed a namespace never becomes the command's exit status. The backend compares against the record and not against the caller's namespaces. When the record says the sandbox shares the host's network namespace, the join skips only the network `setns`: that namespace is owned by the initial user namespace and the join has no capability there, so entering it fails with EPERM. The network inode is still checked against the record, and no other namespace is skipped. A failure at any step exits with a reserved status from one fixed table, one status per step. The parent waits for both forks and maps each status to an error that names the step. The command's exit status, standard output and standard error are returned as Execution."
  FR-004,"Environment: Render",environment-render,"A command receives the merged contributions of the resolved steps, in sequence order, rewritten for the sandbox workspace by `Contributions::environment_for`. The backend adds HOME inside the sandbox, TMPDIR on its temporary filesystem, PWD at the workspace, and PATH. PATH is the contributed path followed by the directories of the baseline utilities. The sandbox has exactly that environment. The launcher starts with an otherwise empty environment, and the renderer emits no per-variable environment flag and no clear-environment flag. No value reaches an argv, and nothing from the caller's environment is inherited or readable through `/proc`. Each command's environment is read from `/proc/<sandbox-pid>/environ`, so a later process needs no file and no value is written to disk or to a log. Provenance records the backend's own variables as set, and inspection reports them."
  FR-005,"Environment: Withhold Credentials",environment-withholding,"The backend withholds a variable whose name matches the credential table before it creates the sandbox. A withheld variable is never carried, so it is never written anywhere. The launcher's environment, the record and every command hold no value for it. The table is data in the backend module and is reviewed with it. It matches lower-case name fragments, and it covers token, secret, password, key and signing names, plus the documented credential variables of Nix, Cargo, npm, GitHub, AWS, GCP, Azure, SSH and GPG. It is not configuration. A project cannot weaken it and a machine cannot extend it in this ADR, because the only use of such an override would be to carry a credential. Adding a family takes one entry and one test. Provenance records a withheld variable as removed and names it, so a build that fails for want of a variable says which variable. The same rule withholds a variable whose value names a host path that the sandbox is not granted. The backend splits the value on the path separator and withholds the variable when an absolute element is neither equal to nor beneath a granted mount. The documentation states the case the table cannot catch. A value the devShell exports under an ordinary name is passed, because the backend recognises the shape of a credential and not the intent of a shell hook."
  FR-006,"Mounts: Validate",mounts-validation,"Before it renders anything, the backend validates every contributed path. The path must resolve beneath the configured store root. It must not resolve to a protected root, the host home, the metadata directory or the backend's state directory. It must not be a socket, device or FIFO. It must not equal, contain or sit inside the workspace. The backend opens the path once, through an already-open directory descriptor and a resolver that refuses symlinks and magic links, and the renderer mounts that descriptor. The object validated is therefore the object the sandbox sees, and a path exchanged between validation and start cannot change the view. The same rule covers the shell, the utilities and the environment bindings. A refused path fails the create and names the contribution and the rule it broke. A read-only bind is not a capability control. A granted tree that holds a unix socket is refused for that reason, because a read-only mount does not stop a connection."
  FR-007,"Mounts: Render",mounts-render,"Each accepted path becomes one read-only bind at its own absolute path, one path at a time, so the view follows the closure and not a hand-maintained list. The workspace is bound read-write at the sandbox workspace path and is the only writable host tree. The private root, the temporary filesystem and the home are writable inside the sandbox and are not host trees. The view also holds what the baseline needs, which is proc, dev, the host system profile where the platform has one, and a shell with the utilities the tools run. Those utilities are cat, grep, timeout and sleep today. The backend resolves them on the host, validates them the way it validates bwrap, and puts them on the sandbox PATH under the names the tools call. A project with no provisioning step therefore has a shell that can evaluate a command. The backend reports both contribution sorts as Available, which is what lets the resolver accept a devshell step for this backend."
  FR-008,"Record: Payload",record-payload,"The backend writes its own `SandboxRecord.data` payload, and every later operation reads it. The payload holds the launcher pid and the sandbox pid with their start times, the seven namespace inodes, the uid the sandbox was created under, the root's device and inode, the mounts from host paths with an access for each, the host workspace directory, the sandbox workspace path, the scope unit when there is one, whether the sandbox is paused, whether it shares the host network, the forwards it holds, and the environment provenance. The payload carries a version. A higher version is refused as unreadable. A lower version, or a payload missing a required field, is refused with a remedy of deleting and recreating the sandbox. The record keeps the metadata store's 0600 file and 0700 directory, and this ADR requires that rather than relying on another module's default. Backend state lives at `data_dir/litterbox/bubblewrap/<project-slug>/<sandbox-name>/`, with the workspace copy inside it. The sandbox workspace path is a backend constant and is recorded in the payload."
  FR-009,"Record: Validate",record-validation,"Before any operation uses a recorded pid, the backend checks it. The pid exists, it is not a zombie, its uid and start time match the record, and all seven namespace inodes match the values recorded at create. Each inode is compared through the same descriptor that setns later receives. The backend checks the root through an open descriptor whose device and inode match the record. It checks that every recorded host path appears at its recorded path with its recorded access, and that no host path outside the record is reachable. The kernel mounts bwrap makes for proc, dev and tmpfs are not host paths and are not part of the comparison. The table holds one entry for each bind (T-035), so a missing entry means the view changed. The outcome depends on the operation. exec, pause, resume, set_limits, upload and download fail as NotFound when the sandbox is absent or has died. inspect and list report Missing instead. delete treats an absent or dead sandbox as already torn down and succeeds. A live pid that fails the uid, identity or mount check fails as Denied, and no join, chroot, signal or cgroup write is attempted. Project and backend ownership is the orchestrator's check. The backend cannot see the caller and does not pretend to make that check."
  FR-010,"Lifecycle: Pause and Resume",lifecycle-facet,"pause and resume stop and continue the sandbox's work without destroying it. The backend records the paused state, so a later process and inspection can report it. With a cgroup scope, pause writes the scope's freeze file and confirms the pause by sampling the namespace's cumulative CPU time until the value stops changing within a fixed window. A frozen task still reports a running state, so progress confirms the pause and process state does not. If the sample never settles, pause reports a failure and thaws the sandbox rather than leaving it in an unknown state. Resume thaws and records the sandbox as running. The backend writes each command into the scope before its exec, and a join that cannot be placed there fails rather than running outside the freeze. Without a scope, and only when the user accepted the degradation, pause stops every member of the sandbox pid namespace with a stop-and-rescan loop, and resume continues every member, including one that appeared after the last scan. A single pass is never used. Freeze and limits manage resources for a cooperative workload. They are not a boundary. Under delegation a workload that runs as the same uid can move itself out of the scope, and the readiness report says so."
  FR-011,"Limits: Cgroup Bounds",resource-limits,"The backend's section accepts a memory ceiling in mebibytes and a task bound. create applies both to the holder's scope. set_limits changes them on a running sandbox through the same scope. No command line command and no MCP tool reaches set_limits in this ADR, so the facet test and the create path cover it. A failed write is reported as a failure. A bound of zero fails as Misconfigured, as it does for Docker, because zero means no bound and would remove the limit the user asked for. Without a scope both refuse with Unsupported that names the cgroup requirement, rather than accepting a bound they cannot enforce."
  FR-012,"Degraded: Without Cgroups",degraded-without-cgroups,"A host without a usable cgroup v2 scope can still create a sandbox, but only because the user accepted the degradation, and the report never says otherwise. Readiness reports the cgroup check as Degraded and carries a fallback entry. The entry names the pid-namespace fallback, says that it bounds neither memory nor task count, and says that a workload running as the same uid can evade either. The fallback entry is required, because the registry only gates a degradation that has one. The backend's capability report replaces the entries that the facets derive for pause and resource limits with that state and fallback, so the machine-wide listing does not offer a bound the host cannot apply. Accepting the degradation changes neither what a join does nor whether the sandbox is bounded."
  FR-013,"Readiness",readiness-checks,"readiness reports one check for each requirement it needs. Each check has a state, a reason and a remedy. The effective uid is not root. The bwrap binary is present, resolves to a canonical absolute path, is root-owned, is not group or world writable, and is at least version 0.8.0. Unprivileged user namespaces are permitted. A cgroup scope is usable for lifecycle and limits, or Degraded with the fallback described above. A holder command resolves on the host. The shell the tools use resolves, with its utilities beside it. No check changes anything, runs a command in a sandbox or starts a holder. create refuses on any check that is not available, except the cgroup one, which it gates behind the user's acceptance. A caller that reaches the backend without the registry's gating still cannot create a sandbox on an unusable host."
  FR-014,"Egress: Configurable",egress-denied,"The backend's section selects one of two network postures. With `network = 'denied'`, which is the default, the sandbox gets its own network namespace: it has no route beyond its own loopback, the host cannot reach a listener inside it, and two commands in the same sandbox reach each other over loopback. With `network = 'host'`, the sandbox shares the host's network namespace: it reaches whatever the host reaches, and a listener it starts is reachable from the host. Inspection reports the posture it actually has, Denied or Direct, and creates no proxy, socket or host route in either case. Sharing the host network is the caller's decision to widen egress; the readiness output and the capability report say which posture is configured."
  FR-015,"Ports: Reported or Refused",ports-refused,"A project that declares ports is refused at create when the sandbox has its own network namespace. The error is Unsupported, and it names the missing requirement, a shared host network, the setting that provides it, and the remedy. With `network = 'host'` the ports are accepted: each is reported as a forward whose host port equals its target, the project's forward bind, and an environment variable carrying the port, so the workload and the port tool agree with what the host can reach. A port is never silently dropped: either it is reachable and reported, or create refuses and names the setting that would allow it. Inspection reports the forwards the record holds."
  FR-016,"Enumeration: Both Scopes",machine-list,"list returns this backend's sandboxes for both scopes. It rebuilds each complete payload from the state directory, so machine-wide pause receives records it can act on. The repository scope matches the project slug. The machine scope covers every project beneath the state root. An entry whose payload cannot be rebuilt is reported as corrupt and is not dropped. A sandbox whose holder died is still listed, with the status inspection reports. On a machine that holds records from both backends, list returns the selected backend's sandboxes, reports the others as belonging to another backend, and does not fail for that reason."
  FR-017,"Delete: Teardown",delete-teardown,"delete checks the record as far as it can, then kills the sandbox pid. That kills every member of its pid namespace, including a command that called setsid. When a scope exists, delete writes the scope's kill file. It waits until the namespace holds no members, and then removes the backend's state directory for that sandbox. It never signals the launcher, which lives in the caller's namespaces and whose death does not end the sandbox. Deleting a sandbox whose holder is already gone succeeds, because the metadata record and the branch are what remain. Nothing outside the sandbox's own state directory and the killed namespace changes. A record that validation refuses is still deletable, because a sandbox has to be removable even when its payload can no longer be read."
  FR-018,"Workspace: Materialise",workspace-materialisation,"The orchestrator hands create a staged workspace in a temporary directory, so the backend copies it into its own state directory before the sandbox starts and binds the copy. The copy, every upload and every download opens each path component without following a link. A symlink, device or FIFO that the sandbox planted is refused and named. Hard links are not refused, because a hard link inside the tree is an ordinary file and no check can tell it from one the tree is meant to hold. upload writes only inside the tree. download reads only from it and copies a directory recursively, preserving modes and keeping a symlink as a symlink, so the orchestrator's snapshot commits the tree and not whatever a link points at. A snapshot that cannot be taken is reported to the caller, not logged and dropped."
  FR-019,"Diagnostics: Three Failures",setup-diagnostics,"The caller can tell a setup failure, a join failure and a workload failure apart. A failure before bwrap creates the sandbox is a create failure that names the stage and carries a bounded excerpt of bwrap's diagnostic. A failure in the join is a reserved status mapped to an error that names the failed step, with the same operation name Docker uses. The workload's own status is returned unchanged. create logs the argv it ran at debug level with every environment value left out. A setup failure reaches the doctor as Unavailable with the requirement named, so the command line exits with the class an unavailable backend already produces."
  FR-020,"Documentation: Configuration",backend-documentation,"The backends page and the user configuration reference name bubblewrap, its keys and units, its readiness output, the fact that it applies a devshell step's environment and closure, what the degraded mode does not bound, and that it refuses a project that declares ports. The page stops describing Docker as the only mechanism. What the documentation says the sandbox can reach matches what inspection reports."
nonFunctional[7]{id,title,slug,description}:
  NFR-001,"Fail Closed",fail-closed,"The backend refuses before a command runs when a condition is unsafe. That covers a bwrap that is missing, not root-owned, below the version floor, or group or world writable; an effective uid of zero; a caller whose uid differs from the recorded one; user namespaces that the host refuses; a record that is unreadable, from a newer version or from an older one; a sandbox pid that is stale or foreign; a contributed path that breaks a validation rule; a link planted in the workspace; a missing holder command; and a missing shell. No path falls back to an unconfined command, and no sandbox starts from a partly validated view. A refusal names the requirement, the reason and a remedy the user can run, and the remedy never suggests running Litterbox as root."
  NFR-002,"Spawned Processes",spawned-processes,"The backend starts bwrap, the holder command, the scope launcher when a scope is used, and the command being run. It starts nothing else. There is no supervisor, no daemon, and no shell that makes a policy decision. It does not use nsenter. run_steps, the create budget, the transfer deadline and the output cap are shared with Docker rather than copied, so the two backends cannot drift. The argv is built from typed values, so no path, variable or argument is split, globbed or interpreted on the way to bwrap."
  NFR-003,"Golden Argv",golden-argv,"One renderer builds bwrap's argv from a typed view. Tests assert the exact vector for the baseline view, for a request with an environment, a two-path closure and a workspace, and for the degraded form without a scope. Each test names the invariants the vector carries. Those are the seven namespaces, the block on nested user namespaces, the absence of a parent-death tie, a private root, a read-only store, the workspace as the only writable host tree, no per-variable environment flag, no clear-environment flag, and the descriptor bind. A second test proves that removing any one of them changes the vector, and that the renderer refuses when one cannot be emitted, so deleting a policy flag means editing a test. Detachment is asserted where it can be seen. The spawn sets all three standard streams to null and asks for no new session."
  NFR-004,"Cost",cost,"A sandbox costs three idle processes, which are the launcher, the pid-1 process bwrap runs and the holder command. A command costs one process tree that exits with it. There is no supervisor. create pays one scope launch and one holder start. The prototype measured 38ms median for a bwrap run against 3.7ms for a bare exec, and 47ms with a scope. A join therefore must not start a second sandbox for each command. Setup time grows with the bind count, 60ms for a three-bind view and 4.2s for 1779 binds, so create pays it once and reports how long the view took (T-035, T-039)."
  NFR-005,"Secrecy",secret-handling,"A contributed value exists in the sandbox's own environment and in the environment of the process that is meant to receive it. It exists nowhere else. It is not in a file, the record, an argv or a log line at any level. Values reach the sandbox through the spawn, and a join reads them back from its environment, so they are never written down. The documentation states what the kernel does not hide. Any process that runs as the same uid can read a process's environment through `/proc`, and the backend cannot change that."
  NFR-006,"Platform Requirements",platform-requirements,"The backend runs on Linux only. It needs bwrap 0.8.0 or newer, unprivileged user namespaces, and a kernel that allows setns to a same-uid process. It needs no root, no daemon, no capability, no kernel module and no virtual machine. It refuses to run with an effective uid of zero rather than acting as if that model held. The registry does not list it on a platform without it, and no other backend changes."
  NFR-007,"Test Coverage",test-coverage,"`backends/bubblewrap/host.rs` defines the seam. One trait covers the host reads and writes the backend needs, which are a pid's existence, state, start time, uid, namespace inodes, environment and mount table; the scope's freeze, thaw, kill and limit files; binary resolution; and a clock. The backend is generic over the trait, and a fake supplies every negative case, so payload validation, readiness and lifecycle are unit-testable without a sandbox. The golden argv test uses the renderer alone. A host suite runs where the requirements exist, and skips with the missing requirement named where they do not. It runs the crate's conformance harness against the backend, which covers duplicate create, upload and download, delete idempotency, inspection after delete, step order and lifecycle visibility. It also covers the cases a fake cannot reach, which are a grandchild's own namespace inodes, a killed client that leaves no zombie, a recycled pid, a foreign bwrap sandbox with a writable root, a namespace the join cannot enter, and a symlink planted in the workspace."
acceptance[17]{id,title,slug,description}:
  AC-001,"Backends Listing",backends-listing,"On this host `litterbox backends` lists bubblewrap beside docker, marks the selected one, and prints each readiness check with its state and, where it is not available, a reason, a remedy and a fallback. `--json` emits the same report with the fields the other backends emit. Those are the backend's name, whether it is selected, one entry for each check with primitive, state, reason, remedy and fallback, and one entry for each capability with operation, state and fallback."
  AC-002,"Create and Shell",create-and-shell,"With a machine configuration that selects bubblewrap, a project that lists it in `[project] backends`, and a devshell step in its provision list, create returns a record whose backend is bubblewrap. `litterbox shell <name> -- uname -s` prints Linux, and `litterbox shell <name> -- cat /proc/self/ns/pid` prints an inode that differs from the host's and equals the one recorded for the sandbox. The first command runs without a retry, because create returned only once the view was built."
  AC-003,"Joined Process Identity",joined-process-identity,"The process that runs the command is a member of the sandbox pid namespace. `litterbox shell <name> -- readlink /proc/self/ns/pid` is read by the exec'd process itself. A command that signals a host pid fails with ESRCH while that host process is still alive, and a command that signals pid 1 reaches the sandbox's own init."
  AC-004,"Shared Context",shared-context,"A listener that one command starts with its standard streams redirected, so the command returns, is reachable from a second command in the same sandbox over loopback, and a connection from the host to that port is refused. Both commands report the same pid-namespace inode."
  AC-005,"Prepared Environment",prepared-environment,"Inside the sandbox a command sees the devShell's variables pointing at the sandbox workspace, and PATH leads with the devShell's directories. A tagged host test that runs `cargo test -p agentkit-common` there succeeds. A variable whose name is in the credential table is absent, inspection reports it as withheld by name, and a variable whose value names an ungranted host path is absent for the same reason."
  AC-006,"Isolation",isolation-holds,"A write to a store path fails read-only. A write into the host home, the metadata directory, the state root or any ungranted host path fails, or the path does not exist. A write to the sandbox home and to `/tmp` succeeds, which proves the baseline is not being mistaken for the boundary. A host credential path does not exist in the view. With the default `network = 'denied'`, an outbound connection fails with no route and the host cannot reach a listener; with `network = 'host'`, egress is Direct and inspection says so. A granted tree that holds a unix socket is refused at create rather than mounted."
  AC-007,"Lifecycle",lifecycle-works,"A command that appends to a file every 50ms stops making progress after pause, inspection reports the sandbox paused, the command continues after resume, inspection reports it running, and a command that called setsid is gone after delete. A pause whose confirmation cannot observe quiescence reports a failure and leaves the sandbox thawed."
  AC-008,"Stale Holder",stale-holder,"Killing the sandbox process out of band makes a command fail with the sandbox not found, inspection reports Missing, and delete succeeds and removes the state directory. A decoy process that reuses the pid is refused by the start-time check."
  AC-009,"Foreign Holder",foreign-holder,"A record whose payload is changed one field at a time is refused with Denied or Unavailable, as the field dictates, and no command runs. The fields are the pid, the start time, the uid, any one namespace inode, the root identity, the recorded mount list and the payload version."
  AC-010,"Ports Refused or Reported",ports-refused-create,"With the default network posture, a project that declares a port is refused at create with Unsupported naming the setting that would allow it, and leaves no branch and no state directory behind. With `network = 'host'`, the same project creates, inspection reports the forward with its host port equal to its target, and the sandbox environment carries the variable that names it."
  AC-011,"Degraded Mode",degraded-accepted,"On a host without a usable user scope, create is refused with the cgroup check named until `accept-degraded = true`. After that, create succeeds, the capability report still says that pause and limits are degraded with their fallback, set_limits refuses at the facet level with Unsupported, and readiness states what is not bounded."
  AC-012,"No Values on Disk",no-values-on-disk,"While a sandbox exists, its record, every file beneath its state directory and its project's metadata file hold no value of any contributed variable and no command text. The captured log of a create, command and delete cycle holds none either. After delete the state directory is gone."
  AC-013,"Docker Unchanged",docker-unchanged,"The existing suite and `arch-lint check` pass, except the two registry tests that name bubblewrap as an absent backend, which move to a name no backend has. DEFAULT stays docker, and no record that one backend wrote is read by the other. A machine that holds records from both backends lists the selected backend's sandboxes, reports the other backend's as another backend's, and does not fail."
  AC-014,"Baseline Userland",baseline-userland,"A project with no provisioning step can evaluate a command string through the command line tool and through the MCP read, write, grep and bash tools, because the baseline binds a shell and the utilities those paths call. Each one resolves on the sandbox PATH under the name it is called by."
  AC-015,"Record Edition Mismatch",record-edition-mismatch,"A record written under a different payload version is refused as Unavailable with the remedy of deleting and recreating the sandbox, and that sandbox can still be deleted."
  AC-016,"Host Join",host-join,"The host suite creates a sandbox, runs two commands, proves the shared pid and loopback context, kills a client and finds no defunct process left behind, and deletes the sandbox. Where bwrap is absent it reports the missing requirement instead of passing."
  AC-017,"Large Closure",large-closure-create,"A project whose store closure is far larger than the probe's 1779 paths creates successfully, create reports how long the view took to build, and the first command runs without a retry. A view that does not finish within the budget fails the create and leaves no state directory."
edgeCases[14]{id,title,slug,description}:
  EC-001,"User Namespaces Blocked",user-namespaces-blocked,"A host whose policy blocks unprivileged user namespaces, as Ubuntu 24.04's defaults do, reports the requirement Unavailable with the sysctl and AppArmor remedy and refuses create. Nothing runs unconfined, and the remedy does not suggest running as root."
  EC-002,"Running as Root",running-as-root,"An effective uid of zero is refused by readiness, create and every command with a Misconfigured error. The same-uid model the join relies on does not hold for a root-owned sandbox, and a root-owned root is escapable in the classic way."
  EC-003,"No Cgroup Delegation",no-cgroup-delegation,"Without a usable user scope the sandbox can still be created once the user accepts the degradation. Pause falls back to a loop that a workload running as the same uid can livelock. Memory and task count are unbounded, and a fork bomb inside the sandbox is charged to the caller's user slice rather than to the sandbox. The report says this in those words."
  EC-004,"Untrusted Bwrap",untrusted-bwrap,"A bwrap that is not root-owned, or that is group or world writable, is skipped by name in favour of a later trusted candidate on the path. A path that holds only untrusted candidates is Unavailable and no bwrap runs. The binary that was validated is the binary that is executed, from the descriptor that was validated."
  EC-005,"Version Floor",version-floor,"A bwrap below 0.8.0 is Unavailable and names the floor and the version it found. A version that lacks a flag the renderer must emit is Unavailable too. The renderer never drops a policy flag for an older binary, and the descriptor bind is written in the form the floor supports."
  EC-006,"Missing Contributed Path",missing-contributed-path,"A closure path that disappeared between prepare and create fails the create and names the path. The backend removes the sandbox and the state directory rather than returning a sandbox that is missing a store path."
  EC-007,"Large Closure",large-closure,"A closure larger than the prototype's 238 paths binds as far as the host allows. A mount failure fails the create and names the path and the store view, with the whole-store view as the remedy. No partial view is ever started."
  EC-008,"Killed Client",killed-client,"A command whose client is killed is reparented outside the pid namespace and reaped there, so no defunct process remains and the sandbox is not charged for it (T-034). The backend needs no reaper of its own, and delete kills the namespace as before."
  EC-009,"Foreign Record",foreign-record,"A pid whose uid or start time differs from the record, or whose namespaces, root or mounts are not the ones recorded, is refused as Denied and never joined. The record is the only authority for what to enter. Ownership by project and backend is the orchestrator's check, which the backend cannot see and does not pretend to make."
  EC-010,"Concurrent Commands",concurrent-commands,"Two commands joined at once share the sandbox, and neither sees the other's output or exit status. A join reads the record and does not write it. Only pause, resume and delete write the record, and they write the whole record."
  EC-011,"Holder Crash",holder-crash,"A sandbox that exits on its own reports Missing, a command against it fails as NotFound, and delete succeeds. Its state directory is left for delete to remove rather than being reaped by a later command that happened to notice."
  EC-012,"Awkward Source Path",awkward-source-path,"A staged workspace whose host path holds spaces or non-ASCII characters copies byte for byte into the state directory, and every path the backend gives bwrap comes from the state root and the sandbox name. The source path never reaches the argv."
  EC-013,"Double Create",double-create,"A create that names a sandbox whose state directory exists, or whose recorded sandbox is alive, fails as Conflict before any process starts, and the existing sandbox and its record are left untouched."
  EC-014,"Paused Then Deleted",paused-then-deleted,"A paused sandbox can be deleted. Teardown thaws or kills through the scope, which a frozen unit cannot do by itself, so delete does not depend on the sandbox being runnable."
